# XSH fclose()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.fclose.001
anchor     functions/fclose.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With "hello" buffered in an output stream (the file still empty),
           fclose() returns 0 and the file holds "hello".
test       t/001-flushes.c

assertion  XSH.fclose.002
anchor     functions/fclose.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       After fclose() of a stream, its descriptor (from fileno()) is no longer
           open.
test       t/002-closes-fd.c

assertion  XSH.fclose.003
anchor     functions/fclose.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       For a 1 MiB file read through a stream (the active handle) with one
           fgetc(), after fclose() a duplicate of the descriptor taken before
           the call has offset 1.
note       The file is 1 MiB, so after one read the stream is not at end
           of file whichever way "not already at EOF" is read (the end-
           of-file indicator or the descriptor's offset; Q-29).
test       t/003-input-offset.c

assertion  XSH.fclose.004
anchor     functions/fclose.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With data buffered in an output stream and the file's times set to
           2001-09-09, after fclose() (2.1 s later) st_mtim is later than that
           and st_ctim later than before the call.
timeout    20
test       t/004-times.c

assertion  XSH.fclose.005
anchor     functions/fclose.html#ERRORS/EBADF
class      required
since      2001
says       With data buffered in a stream whose descriptor was closed,
           fclose() returns EOF with errno EBADF.
test       t/005-ebadf.c

assertion  XSH.fclose.006
anchor     functions/fclose.html#ERRORS/EPIPE
class      required
since      2001
says       With SIGPIPE blocked and data buffered in a stream on a pipe whose read
           end is closed, fclose() returns EOF with errno EPIPE, and SIGPIPE
           is pending.
test       t/006-epipe.c

assertion  XSH.fclose.007
anchor     functions/fclose.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       When fclose() of a stream on a pipe with no reader fails (EPIPE), the
           stream's descriptor is closed all the same.
test       t/007-closed-on-error.c

assertion  XSH.fclose.008
anchor     functions/fclose.html#ERRORS/EINTR
class      required
since      2001
says       With a stream on a full pipe (blocking) holding 1000 buffered bytes,
           a SIGALRM caught without SA_RESTART 1 s into fclose() makes it
           return EOF with errno EINTR (later alarms drain the pipe, so an
           implementation that restarts finishes and is reported).
timeout    20
test       t/008-eintr.c

assertion  XSH.fclose.009
anchor     functions/fclose.html#ERRORS/EAGAIN
class      required
since      2001
says       With a pipe filled to capacity and O_NONBLOCK set on its write
           end, fclose() of a stream on that end holding 1000 buffered bytes
           returns EOF with errno EAGAIN.
test       t/009-eagain.c

assertion  XSH.fclose.010
anchor     functions/fclose.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       For a 1 MiB file opened "r+" with one fgetc(), after fclose() a
           duplicate of the descriptor taken before the call has offset 1.
note       The file is 1 MiB, so after one read the stream is not at end
           of file whichever way "not already at EOF" is read (the end-
           of-file indicator or the descriptor's offset; Q-29).
test       t/010-update-input.c

assertion  XSH.fclose.011
anchor     functions/fclose.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       For a 1 MiB file read through a stream with fgetc() twice and then
           ungetc('x') (stream position 1), after fclose() a duplicate of the
           descriptor has offset 1.
note       The file is 1 MiB, so after one read the stream is not at end
           of file whichever way "not already at EOF" is read (the end-
           of-file indicator or the descriptor's offset; Q-29).
test       t/011-pushback-offset.c

assertion  XSH.fclose.012
anchor     functions/fclose.html#ERRORS/ENOSPC
class      required
since      2008
says       For a stream from fmemopen() on a 4-byte buffer opened "w" and given
           a 16 KiB buffer of its own, after fputs() of 10 bytes fclose()
           returns EOF with errno ENOSPC (where fputs() itself already
           fails, the assertion is UNRESOLVED).
test       t/012-enospc-fmemopen.c

assertion  XSH.fclose.013
anchor     functions/fclose.html#ERRORS/EFBIG
class      required
since      2024
says       In a child whose soft RLIMIT_FSIZE is 4096 bytes (SIGXFSZ ignored),
           fclose() of a stream holding 8192 buffered bytes returns EOF with
           errno EFBIG.
test       t/013-efbig.c
